  [3mBuys:[0m npm dependencies work in the browser, and you get source maps — which matter for symbolicating minified stack
  traces in a hosted service. [3mCosts:[0m a build step, a devDependency, and a [38;5;153mdist/[39m artifact in a repo that currently has
  zero tooling; deploying now means building first. [3mRules out:[0m nothing, but it's hard to walk back once the deploy path
  assumes it.

  [1mC. One UMD-style file, zero module conversion[0m — a single [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m with a UMD wrapper that works both as a classic
  script and under [38;5;153mrequire()[39m, branching on environment internally.
  [3mBuys:[0m the smallest possible diff — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and both entry points stay exactly as they are. [3mCosts:[0m runtime branching
  lives [3minside[0m the module, which is precisely the coupling the transport seam exists to prevent; it's awkward to test,
  and the browser still needs a vendor script tag anyway. [3mRules out:[0m clean per-runtime testing, mostly.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m Your stated problem is that errors vanish, and the thing that fixes it is global handlers plus a
  real sink — both of which are runtime-specific. A puts that specificity in two small adapters and keeps the part
  worth sharing (levels, event shape, redaction) genuinely shared and testable without a network or a DOM. B is the
  right answer the day you minify or need browser npm deps; buying the build step before then is cost without return. C
  wins only if the ESM conversion is somehow off-limits — and for three files this size, it isn't.

  Cheap to change later: level names, event fields, which service. Expensive: B's build step, once deploys depend on it.
